1
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:04,530
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,800
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:12,370
My name is Catherine and today we have a lesson for you all about life at home and life with your parents.

4
00:00:12,870 --> 00:00:13,350
That's right.

5
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:23,490
We've all lived with our parents when we were younger or maybe we still do and of course they have certain rules in the house and well, when you break those rules, something happens.

6
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:27,260
There are consequences and that's what's happening in today's dialogue.

7
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:33,370
So let's take a listen to this dialogue and when we come back, we'll be talking more about what's going on and what some of these words and phrases mean.

8
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,700
Do you know what time it is?

9
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:48,750
Um, ten.

10
00:00:49,430 --> 00:00:50,710
Get in this door, young man.

11
00:00:51,090 --> 00:00:51,590
It's midnight.

12
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:53,830
You are two hours past curfew.

13
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:55,990
I know, but it wasn't my fault.

14
00:00:56,390 --> 00:01:00,090
I told Jennifer she had to drop me off before ten, but she wouldn't leave the party.

15
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:01,920
I don't care.

16
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:03,690
You are grounded for life, mister.

17
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:06,010
Mom, that is so unfair.

18
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:08,850
You know the rules and you broke them.

19
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:11,650
No allowance and no TV for a week.

20
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:16,290
I usually never ground you, but this time I have to put my foot down.

21
00:01:16,670 --> 00:01:16,990
What?

22
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:19,270
For being a couple of hours late?

23
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:20,950
You have to be kidding.

24
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:22,590
I don't want to hear it.

25
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:23,750
Now go to your room.

26
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:30,480
All right, we're back.

27
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,040
So a very typical case, somebody or the kid returns home, it's too late and now they're in trouble.

28
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,040
Yes, his parents are not very happy.

29
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:43,720
In this case, it's his mom who is telling him what the consequences will be.

30
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:47,400
So let's take a look at Language Takeaway and find out what some of these key words are.

31
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:49,970
Language Takeaway.

32
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:59,840
All right, so the first word that we're going to take a look is actually something that his mom said and he said that you are two hours past curfew.

33
00:01:59,970 --> 00:02:00,390
Curfew.

34
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:05,910
So curfew has to do specifically with the time when your parents expect you at home.

35
00:02:06,390 --> 00:02:06,790
That's right.

36
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:12,600
So if your curfew is ten o'clock, you have to be home before or by ten o'clock.

37
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:13,020
Right.

38
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:20,700
So in this case, if the boy's curfew is ten o'clock and he arrives home at midnight, twelve o'clock, he's two hours past curfew.

39
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:21,600
That's right.

40
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:27,840
And even sometimes cities or governments will implement certain curfews at certain times.

41
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:28,000
That's right.

42
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:31,060
And I don't think adults really have curfews at home.

43
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:32,320
No, I don't think so.

44
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:34,720
It depends on what you want, but kids definitely have curfews.

45
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:37,560
What happens though, Marco, when you break curfew?

46
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:42,220
So this is a very serious offense and you're going to get punished.

47
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:50,440
And the way that we say this, usually parents to their children, you are grounded or you ground that kid.

48
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:51,160
Okay.

49
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:55,340
So you might have heard this word when talking about airplanes or flights at an airport.

50
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:56,780
The plane was grounded.

51
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:59,200
It basically means it can't go anywhere.

52
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:06,320
And so when you're talking about a kid or a person, you basically mean, okay, you're grounded, you can't go out with your friends.

53
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,260
You can't go anywhere.

54
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:09,000
Usually you can go to school.

55
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:09,800
Right, right.

56
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:11,720
But you can't have fun is the meaning.

57
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:12,400
That's right.

58
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:14,420
So it's usually a fixed phrase.

59
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:15,260
You're grounded.

60
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:16,780
That means that you are punished.

61
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:17,780
You can't go out.

62
00:03:18,230 --> 00:03:21,390
But in this case, the mom specified saying you're grounded.

63
00:03:22,190 --> 00:03:23,770
So that means that he can't go out.

64
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:28,110
But on top of that, she said no allowance and no TV for a week.

65
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:29,330
Oh man.

66
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,230
So allowance is the money that your parents give you to go out, like to go to the movies or something.

67
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:39,830
And your parents might give this to you on a weekly basis.

68
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:44,830
So every Saturday you get $5 or on a monthly basis, every month you get $20.

69
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:52,880
And when his mother says you're not going to have any allowance for a week, that means we're not going to give you any money this week.

70
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:53,110
That's right.

71
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:57,350
So that is an allowance, how much money you get per month or per week.

72
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,590
The key thing about allowance though, is that it's not something you do for work.

73
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,250
You don't work and receive allowance.

74
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:05,110
If you work, you have a salary.

75
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,970
Allowance is something that you get for free from someone.

76
00:04:08,120 --> 00:04:14,640
So you can get an allowance from your boss to eat, but you can also have an allowance from your parents to do fun things.

77
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:15,640
That's right.

78
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:21,610
So usually kids, obviously they can't work, so they get an allowance from their parents, usually for fun things like going to the movies and stuff.

79
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,200
Exactly.

80
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,200
All right.

81
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:31,480
Now, going back a little bit, let's take a look at two ways that the mom addressed or called her son, because obviously it's her son.

82
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:34,740
But when he got home, she said, get in this door, young man.

83
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:36,160
Okay.

84
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,000
So, angry mom phrases.

85
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,600
When you're in trouble, your parents tend to use different words to talk to you.

86
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:46,420
So this is a very popular one, young man.

87
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:50,400
So it's like saying, you should be mature, but you made a mistake.

88
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:51,840
All right.

89
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,240
So, come inside right now, young man.

90
00:04:56,160 --> 00:05:00,260
It's a way to emphasize that you are also very angry.

91
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:04,360
And also, when he was trying to explain what happened, she said, I don't care.

92
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:06,940
You're grounded for life, mister.

93
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:09,140
You're grounded for life, mister.

94
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:12,150
So these are both ways of talking to young boys.

95
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,440
Mister is the opposite of miss.

96
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:19,770
But in the case of a young girl, you'd say, get in this door, young lady.

97
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:21,240
Right.

98
00:05:21,240 --> 00:05:23,010
And then, you're grounded for life, missy.

99
00:05:23,330 --> 00:05:23,810
Missy.

100
00:05:24,010 --> 00:05:25,130
Yeah, I think they say missy.

101
00:05:25,370 --> 00:05:25,650
Missy.

102
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:27,870
So, missy, mister, young man, young lady.

103
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:32,600
These are all ways of referring to people in kind of, it's weird.

104
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:34,130
It's kind of like they're adults, but they're not.

105
00:05:34,290 --> 00:05:34,910
Yeah, that's right.

106
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:36,090
That's like saying, you're in trouble.

107
00:05:36,410 --> 00:05:36,570
Yeah.

108
00:05:37,050 --> 00:05:41,490
So, very interesting things going on here with this young man who got grounded.

109
00:05:42,010 --> 00:05:45,210
Why don't we go back, let's take a look at our dialogue again, and we'll be back in a bit.

110
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:00,900
Do you know what time it is?

111
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,720
10.

112
00:06:03,720 --> 00:06:04,880
Get in this door, young man.

113
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:05,760
It's midnight.

114
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:07,980
You are two hours past curfew.

115
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:10,160
I know, but it wasn't my fault.

116
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:14,260
I told Jennifer she had to drop me off before 10, but she wouldn't leave the party.

117
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:16,080
I don't care.

118
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,810
You are grounded for life, mister.

119
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,170
Mom, that is so unfair.

120
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:22,990
You know the rules, and you broke them.

121
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:25,810
No allowance and no TV for a week.

122
00:06:26,390 --> 00:06:30,450
I usually never ground you, but this time I have to put my foot down.

123
00:06:30,810 --> 00:06:31,130
What?

124
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:34,200
You're being a couple of hours late?

125
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:35,090
You have to be kidding.

126
00:06:35,690 --> 00:06:36,750
I don't want to hear it.

127
00:06:37,030 --> 00:06:37,910
Now, go to your room.

128
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:41,600
All right, we're back.

129
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:48,520
So, now let's take a look at four key phrases that we've picked out for you on Fluency Builder.

130
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:48,800
Fluency Builder.

131
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:50,520
All right.

132
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:58,440
So, this first phrase is something that actually we hear when the young boy or the young man is giving his excuse for being late.

133
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:02,360
He said, listen, I told Jennifer she had to drop me off before 10.

134
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:03,060
That's right.

135
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:05,820
So, the phrasal verb there is to drop off.

136
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:09,920
This means to leave someone somewhere.

137
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:10,600
That's right.

138
00:07:11,160 --> 00:07:14,520
But usually it means to drive someone to a certain place.

139
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:23,640
So, for example, Jennifer probably has a car, and Jennifer was supposed to take this boy home, so he said she was going to drop me off before 10 o'clock.

140
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:25,360
That means kind of leave him at his house.

141
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:26,060
That's right.

142
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:30,040
The verb is to drop off, but we usually insert the subject in between it.

143
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:31,040
So, to drop me off.

144
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:32,780
She is going to drop me off.

145
00:07:33,020 --> 00:07:34,620
I dropped him off at the bank.

146
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:35,380
That's right.

147
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:36,300
All right.

148
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:48,100
So, now that Jennifer was supposed to drop him off at 10, that didn't happen, so he got grounded and his mom was saying that she usually doesn't really ground him, but this time I have to put my foot down.

149
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:49,100
She's angry.

150
00:07:49,480 --> 00:07:49,600
Yeah.

151
00:07:50,440 --> 00:07:53,520
He's upset and she's angry and she said, listen, I have to put my foot down.

152
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:54,180
What does this mean?

153
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:55,980
You put your foot on the ground?

154
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:57,680
It's strange to hear this phrase.

155
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:03,560
It means that she's going to be strict, that she's not going to let him just get away with it or not ground him.

156
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:05,860
She really has to be strict and she has to punish him.

157
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:06,360
Okay.

158
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:13,280
So, to put your foot down or I put my foot down, this means to be strict with someone, to not maybe bend the rules or be flexible.

159
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:17,790
So, for example, my mother was always very, very loose with my brother.

160
00:08:17,930 --> 00:08:23,230
He could do what he wanted or whatever, but one day he did something really horrible and she finally put her foot down.

161
00:08:23,330 --> 00:08:24,070
And she grounded him.

162
00:08:24,070 --> 00:08:25,450
She grounded him for three months.

163
00:08:26,360 --> 00:08:26,630
Alright.

164
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,050
So, that's what happens when somebody puts their foot down.

165
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:36,330
And now, obviously, he was very surprised that he's going to be grounded for so long and only for being a couple of hours late.

166
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:38,810
So, he said, you have to be kidding.

167
00:08:39,150 --> 00:08:40,590
You have got to be kidding.

168
00:08:40,930 --> 00:08:42,610
So, this is a rhetorical.

169
00:08:42,770 --> 00:08:45,350
So, he's not talking about joking here.

170
00:08:45,470 --> 00:08:47,110
He's saying, no way.

171
00:08:47,510 --> 00:08:48,810
I can't believe it.

172
00:08:48,930 --> 00:08:50,530
This is not happening to me.

173
00:08:50,650 --> 00:08:50,790
Yeah.

174
00:08:51,570 --> 00:08:52,610
You're not serious.

175
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:53,680
You're not serious.

176
00:08:53,770 --> 00:08:54,710
And I would be too.

177
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:55,910
No one likes to be grounded.

178
00:08:56,750 --> 00:08:58,150
But he's saying, okay, come on.

179
00:08:58,600 --> 00:08:58,870
No way.

180
00:08:59,600 --> 00:08:59,770
That's too serious.

181
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:01,600
That's right.

182
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:07,640
So, this is a phrase that you can use with someone if you're not really believing what they're saying or you're surprised.

183
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:08,630
You have to be kidding me.

184
00:09:08,810 --> 00:09:09,010
Yeah.

185
00:09:09,030 --> 00:09:11,350
But usually, there's also a degree of anger.

186
00:09:11,890 --> 00:09:13,230
So, you're upset at the decision.

187
00:09:13,690 --> 00:09:17,490
For example, I'm watching the World Cup right now and there was a foul.

188
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:22,900
And I was very upset because I didn't think it was a foul and I said, you have got to be kidding me.

189
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:23,480
That's right.

190
00:09:23,780 --> 00:09:24,160
Okay.

191
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:27,220
So, that's exactly the way that we would use that phrase.

192
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:30,320
And well, obviously, the mother had made up her mind.

193
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:31,300
She's very decided.

194
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:33,360
And she said, I don't want to hear it.

195
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:35,720
So, this is the end of the conversation.

196
00:09:36,040 --> 00:09:36,260
Okay.

197
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:40,300
This is one of those phrases that will just stop anything you're talking about.

198
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:43,580
So, they're arguing and she says, nope, I don't want to hear it.

199
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:45,060
That means no more excuses.

200
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:46,480
No more talking.

201
00:09:46,960 --> 00:09:47,420
This is the end.

202
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:48,100
That's right.

203
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:49,040
I don't want to hear it.

204
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
And it's used in many scenarios, right?

205
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,640
Not only for somebody being angry or grounded, but also in a meeting.

206
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:55,240
I don't want to hear it.

207
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:55,580
That's it.

208
00:09:55,780 --> 00:09:56,940
We're doing my idea.

209
00:09:57,200 --> 00:09:58,100
We're going forward with it.

210
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:00,480
Well, be careful because that's usually what the boss will say.

211
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:01,200
That's right.

212
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:03,900
It's not the most gentle phrase.

213
00:10:04,040 --> 00:10:06,120
And so, you have to be careful with how you use it.

214
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:11,340
But if you have authority like you're a parent or you're a boss, you can say, nope, I don't want to hear it.

215
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:13,020
I have made my decision.

216
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:13,720
That's right.

217
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:15,520
Okay.

